/** @page overview-bicop Bivariate copula models

@tableofcontents

- [Implemented bivariate copula families](#bicop-families)
- [Set up a custom bivariate copula model](#bicop-custom)
- [Fit and select a bivariate copula](#bicop-fit)
- [Work with a bivariate copula model](#bicop-model)
- [Dependence measures](#bicop-dependence)
- [Per-observation parameters](#bicop-per-row)
- [Serialization](#bicop-serialization)

Bivariate copula models are implemented as the `Bicop` class, and `BicopFamily`
is a closely related enum class describing the type or "family" of copula.
To use bivariate copula models in your code, include the header
`vinecopulib/bicop/class.hpp` (or simply `vinecopulib.hpp`) at the top of
your source file.

Discrete variables are covered in @ref discrete, and the score, gradient and
Hessian of the log-likelihood in @ref inference.

@section bicop-families Implemented bivariate copula families

| type          | name                  | BicopFamily | parameters |
|---------------|-----------------------|-------------|------------|
| -             | Independence          | `indep`     | 0 |
| Elliptical    | Gaussian              | `gaussian`  | 1 |
|               | Student t             | `student`   | 2 |
| Archimedean   | Clayton               | `clayton`   | 1 |
|               | Gumbel                | `gumbel`    | 1 |
|               | Frank                 | `frank`     | 1 |
|               | Joe                   | `joe`       | 1 |
|               | BB1                   | `bb1`       | 2 |
|               | BB6                   | `bb6`       | 2 |
|               | BB7                   | `bb7`       | 2 |
|               | BB8                   | `bb8`       | 2 |
| Extreme-Value | Tawn                  | `tawn`      | 3 |
| Nonparametric | Transformation kernel | `tll`       | (a grid) |

Each family's parameter ranges, admissible rotations and tail behavior are
documented on the `BicopFamily` enum itself.

Several convenience vectors of families live in the sub-namespace
`bicop_families`, and are the intended way to constrain a search space:

| vector | contents |
|--------|----------|
| `all` | every family |
| `parametric` | all except `tll` |
| `nonparametric` | `indep` and `tll` |
| `one_par` | `gaussian`, `clayton`, `gumbel`, `frank`, `joe` |
| `two_par` | `student`, `bb1`, `bb6`, `bb7`, `bb8` |
| `three_par` | `tawn` |
| `elliptical` | `gaussian`, `student` |
| `archimedean` | `clayton`, `gumbel`, `frank`, `joe`, `bb1`, `bb6`, `bb7`, `bb8` |
| `extreme_value` | `tawn`, `gumbel` |
| `bb` | `bb1`, `bb6`, `bb7`, `bb8` |
| `rotationless` | `indep`, `gaussian`, `student`, `frank`, `tll` — they already cover both signs of dependence |
| `lt` | stronger lower-tail dependence: `clayton`, `bb1`, `bb7`, `tawn` |
| `ut` | stronger upper-tail dependence: `gumbel`, `joe`, `bb1`, `bb6`, `bb7`, `bb8`, `tawn` |
| `itau` | estimation by Kendall's \f$ \tau \f$ inversion is available: `indep`, `gaussian`, `student`, `clayton`, `gumbel`, `frank`, `joe` |
| `analytic_derivs` | closed-form density and h-function derivatives; other parametric families fall back to finite differences |

@snippet bicop.cpp families

@section bicop-custom Set up a custom bivariate copula model

There are essentially two ways of setting up a bivariate copula:
- with known parameters,
- from data (i.e., with estimated parameters).

The constructor with known parameters takes four arguments, all defaulted:
- the copula family (default `indep`),
- the rotation, one of `0`, `90`, `180`, `270` (default `0`),
- the parameters (default: those of an independence copula),
- the variable types (default `{"c", "c"}`, i.e. both continuous).

@snippet bicop.cpp custom

The constructor from data takes the same arguments as `select()` and is
described in the next section.

@section bicop-fit Fit and select a bivariate copula

You can either fit the parameters of a given `Bicop` object with `fit()`, or
select the best fitting model from a set of families with `select()`.

@snippet bicop.cpp fit

As it is arguably the most important function of the `Bicop` class, it is worth
understanding the second argument of `select()`, an object of the class
`FitControlsBicop`:

- `std::vector<BicopFamily> family_set` — the set of families to select from.
  Takes a user-specified vector or any of the vectors above (default
  `bicop_families::all`).
- `std::string parametric_method` — `"mle"` (default) for maximum likelihood, or
  `"itau"` for Kendall's \f$ \tau \f$ inversion (only for families in
  `bicop_families::itau`).
- `std::string nonparametric_method` — the degree of the density approximation
  for the transformation kernel estimator: `"constant"` (default), `"linear"` or
  `"quadratic"`.
- `double nonparametric_mult` — a factor multiplying the smoothing parameters.
- `size_t nonparametric_grid_size` — the size of the interpolation grid backing
  `tll` (default `30`).
- `std::string selection_criterion` — `"loglik"`, `"aic"` (default), `"bic"` or
  `"mbic"`.
- `Eigen::VectorXd weights` — optional observation weights.
- `double psi0` — the prior probability of non-independence used by `"mbic"`
  (default `0.9`).
- `bool preselect_families` — a heuristic preselection (default `true`) based on
  symmetry properties of the data; the unrotated Clayton is not preselected when
  the data show upper-tail dependence, for instance.
- `bool allow_rotations` — whether rotations may be selected (default `true`).
- `size_t num_threads` — threads used when fitting several families.

@warning `nonparametric_grid_size` was inserted before `selection_criterion` in
release 1.0.0. Code that passed these positionally must be revisited; see
@ref migrating-to-1-0.

As mentioned [above](#bicop-custom), the arguments of `select()` can be given to
a constructor, instantiating a fitted object directly:

@snippet bicop.cpp select-controls

@section bicop-model Work with a bivariate copula model

You can simulate from a model, and evaluate its density, distribution function,
h-functions, inverse h-functions, log-likelihood and information criteria.

@snippet bicop.cpp model

@section bicop-dependence Dependence measures

`get_tau()` reports Kendall's \f$ \tau \f$ of the current parameters, and
`parameters_to_tau()` does the same for an arbitrary parametrization of the
current family. The inverse, `tau_to_parameters()`, only exists where \f$ \tau
\f$ determines the parameters completely — see its documentation for the exact
set of families.

`get_beta()` returns Blomqvist's beta, and `get_taildep()` a \f$ 2 \times 2 \f$
matrix whose \f$ (0,0) \f$ and \f$ (1,1) \f$ entries are the lower and upper
tail-dependence coefficients (`NaN` for families with no closed form).

@snippet bicop.cpp dependence

@section bicop-per-row Per-observation parameters

For parametric families, `pdf()`, `cdf()`, `hfunc1()`, `hfunc2()`, `hinv1()`,
`hinv2()`, `loglik()`, `simulate()` and the derivative methods also have
overloads taking one parameter set per observation, as in conditional or
covariate-dependent copula models.

@snippet bicop.cpp per-row

@section bicop-serialization Serialization

Bivariate copula models can be written to and constructed from JSON or CBOR
files and [`nlohmann::json` objects](https://github.com/nlohmann/json). CBOR is
a binary encoding of exactly the same logical representation: it is generally
substantially smaller and faster to parse, while JSON remains useful for
inspection and interchange. A `.cbor` extension selects CBOR; any other
extension is JSON.

@snippet bicop.cpp serialization

*/
